(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-26":{"number":26,"title":"Second Verse","content":"Greg had written a second verse on the back of a receipt.\n\nThe first verse filled nearly a page of his notebook. The second consisted of three crossed-out words, a coffee stain, and a drawing of what might have been a sad guitar. I studied it while he adjusted the recording levels.\n\n\"Powerful,\" I said.\n\n\"It's a work in progress.\"\n\n\"The sad guitar moved me.\"\n\n\"That's a self-portrait.\"\n\nThe garage was crowded with equipment, half the cables labeled and the other half apparently living by instinct. A blue light on the cassette recorder blinked at us. Outside, a delivery truck went past, rattling the window hard enough to shake a loose pencil off the shelf.\n\nWe'd agreed to spend the afternoon working on something new. I hadn't understood how difficult new could be for somebody whose older songs still carried so much of his life.\n\nGreg tested a chord, stopped, and started it again.\n\n\"Does it sound too much like something I used to play?\" he asked.\n\n\"Which something?\"\n\nHe didn't answer right away.\n\nI sat on the floor beside the mixing desk. \"You can say Rose's name.\"\n\n\"I know. Sometimes I worry that if I bring her into a room, you won't have enough space left in it.\"\n\n\"That's a pretty crowded room you've imagined.\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck. \"Yeah.\"\n\nI reached for the notebook, then stopped. \"Can I write an idea down?\"\n\n\"Please. Save me from the guitar face.\"\n\nI wrote a sentence about a road that changed under the wheels without pretending the places behind us hadn't been real. It wasn't a lyric yet. It was something we could disagree about.\n\nGreg read it. \"That feels too neat.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"Roads don't always take you someplace better. Sometimes you just take a different turn.\"\n\n\"Then write that.\"\n\nHe picked up the pencil. Half a minute later he was laughing.\n\n\"Great. I made it depressing.\"\n\n\"You made it true.\"\n\nWe worked for an hour and produced a spectacularly bad bridge, which wasn't a good sign for a song or the people singing it. Greg played one version with so much enthusiasm that a loose speaker vibrated off its stand. I caught the cable before it dragged the recorder with it.\n\n\"That's it,\" I said. \"The equipment is voting no.\"\n\nGreg looked mournfully at the stand. \"Critics.\"\n\nWe took a break to repair it. He showed me how to wrap a cable properly rather than letting me pretend I knew. I was grateful he didn't turn the lesson into a demonstration of how talented he was; he made mistakes, too, and admitted when he'd mislabeled a channel.\n\nThe repaired stand held while Greg tuned the guitar again. I wanted to applaud the equipment simply for staying where it belonged.\n\n\"You're treating that cable like it's personally betrayed you,\" I said.\n\n\"It knows what it did.\"\n\nHe pulled the recorder's old label off and replaced it with one showing the channel numbers. The garage had a thousand tiny signs of being used by somebody who'd once had to carry everything alone: a spare string tucked under a mug, tools in the guitar case, a good microphone wrapped in an old shirt. Being able to afford better equipment hadn't taught him how to stop bracing for it to break.\n\nI asked whether Steven might want to hear the new song when it was ready.\n\n\"Maybe,\" Greg said. \"I don't want him to think I'm trying to write something about his mom without asking.\"\n\n\"Then don't say it's about her unless you mean it.\"\n\nGreg looked at the receipt in his hand. \"The first verse came from me thinking about someone who isn't here. The second keeps trying to turn into a promise to somebody who's right in front of me.\"\n\n\"Do you think a promise has to be the end of the song?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nI felt a little foolish admitting that I didn't either. We had a calendar with several people's names on it, different plans in different places, and an agreement that affection wasn't a contest. None of that supplied a rhyme.\n\n\"Could the next verse be about what you do when you're afraid?\" I asked.\n\n\"Probably a terrible song. Mostly I check the tires on the van.\"\n\n\"Then maybe that's where it starts.\"\n\nHe laughed. Then he wrote down *check the tires* in the notebook, shook his head, and crossed it out. A moment later he put it back.\n\nWe found a rhythm in the sound of the laundry dryer next door, a small uneven thump that landed just after the beat. Greg tried to copy it on the guitar and ended up inventing a melody neither of us recognized.\n\n\"Do you like that?\" I asked.\n\nHe played it again.\n\n\"I do. Even if nobody else ever hears it.\"\n\nThat seemed to frighten him more than the possibility of a bad review. He had spent years practicing for an audience, even when he said he didn't care.\n\nI sat on the edge of the workbench, close but not close enough to touch him by accident. He put the guitar down for a moment and reached for my hand.\n\n\"Thanks for letting a song be just a song,\" he said.\n\n\"You're welcome. Although I reserve the right to judge the sad-guitar drawing.\"\n\n\"It's my best portrait.\"\n\nWe laughed so hard we had to start the recording again.\n\nAs he retightened a knob, he said, \"I used to think the way to prove I meant something was to turn every feeling into a song.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I'm trying to work out whether I can just tell you stuff.\"\n\n\"You can.\"\n\n\"Okay.\" He set the screwdriver down. \"I miss her. Sometimes I miss the version of me who didn't know how complicated everything would get. And I really, really like being here with you.\"\n\nI felt my chest tighten.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me.\"\n\n\"That's it?\"\n\n\"Did you want a drum solo?\"\n\nHe laughed. Then he looked at me with genuine uncertainty.\n\nI moved closer. \"I've told you I prefer polyamory, that I'm ethically non-monogamous. I love different people in different ways. It doesn't mean I want each relationship to prove it can replace the others. I don't want you to erase Rose to make room for me. And I don't need to erase the people I care about when I'm with you.\"\n\nHe took a breath. \"So I can write a song that isn't a competition.\"\n\n\"Please. Competition songs sound exhausting.\"\n\nThe cassette recorder clicked at the end of the tape.\n\nGreg stared at it. “Great timing. Even the machine's telling me I'm overthinking.”\n\n“Is that a diagnosis?”\n\n“Professional musician's opinion. Very expensive. First consultation's free.”\n\nHe rewound the tape, and the motor made a strained squeak. I smelled dust warming inside the casing. The recorder belonged to a local woman who had agreed to lend it on the condition that we return it before her daughter's rehearsal. Greg looked at the clock and winced.\n\n“We've got forty minutes,” he said.\n\n“Then let's not pretend we can record a whole album.”\n\nHe laughed, then stopped with his hand on the play button. “What if the verse still isn't right?”\n\n“Then it's a draft.”\n\nHe knew that. But he'd been holding the song up against the songs that were already part of his life, the ones everybody associated with Rose, with Steven, with a younger man whose dreams had changed shape. I couldn't settle that comparison by loving him harder.\n\nI sat beside the mixer and took out the pencil he'd dropped earlier.\n\n“Play me the two versions that sound most different,” I said.\n\n“Which one do you hate?”\n\n“That's a terrible way to ask.”\n\n“Yeah,” he said. “Old habit.”\n\nHe played a cautious verse full of reassurance and another with a crooked little pause after the line about the van's tires. I liked the pause. It sounded like someone discovering he could stop and still keep going.\n\n“Can we leave space there?” I asked.\n\nGreg listened again. Then he gave me the sort of smile he usually reserved for an unexpectedly good chord.\n\n“That might be the only part that knows what it's doing.”\n\nWe recorded a rough take, labeled the cassette with the actual date, and packed the machine before its owner had to come looking. Greg put the returned equipment receipt in his notebook, tucked beside the new verse.\n\nFor once he didn't apologize to the song for being unfinished.\n\n\nWe went back to the second verse. It was rougher now, less clever. He played it once, stopped, and revised two notes. When he let me hear the full song again, the new part didn't explain the old one away. It let the melody move forward.\n\nWe had to finish packing the instruments before dinner. Someone was borrowing the room the next morning, and there was a real schedule to honor. I labeled the last cable and watched Greg put the notebook into the case instead of leaving it where the damp could curl its pages.\n\nAt the door, he stopped.\n\n\"Could I kiss you before we leave?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was a little clumsy because his guitar case was wedged between us. He put it down. I pulled him close, laughing when he nearly tripped over the strap. For a moment the garage smelled only of dust, warm electronics, and him.\n\nHe shut off the recording light.\n\nWe didn't finish the arrangement that night. We did finish talking. When I came back the following week, the notebook was still open to the second verse, and Greg had penciled one extra word in the margin: *again*.","word_count":1614},"su-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Shape of Sorry","content":"The pasta had turned into a single damp opinion about disappointment.\n\nI'd cooked it because Greg said he'd be home for dinner. I had even put the little battery candles on the table, since I knew we'd both end up knocking over real ones. At eight, the food was cold. At nine, I stopped looking at the clock and started doing the dishes.\n\nAt ten, he came through the door with wet flowers and a broken umbrella.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said. \"I have a really bad explanation.\"\n\n\"That makes two of us. I have really bad pasta.\"\n\nHe tried to laugh. I couldn't.\n\nGreg lowered the flowers.\n\nHe had been helping a neighbor whose van wouldn't start. One repair had become another, then a trip to find the right belt, then a long drive back. The neighbor was safe. Greg had his phone. He just hadn't called because every time he looked up, he thought he'd be done in five more minutes.\n\n\"Did you think I wouldn't notice?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I thought I could still make it.\"\n\n\"At ten?\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I know.\"\n\nI didn't want to be cruel. I also didn't want to turn my hurt into a little smile that would teach him there was no cost to letting me wait.\n\n\"Can we sit down?\" I asked.\n\nHe took the chair opposite me. The flowers dripped onto the table. He put them in the sink, then sat again without asking whether that made everything better.\n\n\"I was excited about tonight,\" I said. \"Not because you were supposed to prove something. Just because I'd saved the evening for us.\"\n\n\"I was excited too.\"\n\n\"Then why was I the last person you thought to tell?\"\n\nHe looked at his hands.\n\n\"Because that neighbor was right there asking me for help,\" he said. \"You weren't. And I thought if I called, I'd have to say I was choosing something else instead of you.\"\n\n\"Not calling was a choice too.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nThe apology came slowly. Not dramatic, not lyrical. He said he'd left me waiting, that I had every reason to be upset, and that he wouldn't make me responsible for reassuring him while he apologized.\n\nI nearly cried at that. I hated how much a plain sentence could matter.\n\n\"Are you asking me to forgive you now?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I'd like it eventually. But right now I'm trying to listen.\"\n\nWe reheated what was left of the pasta and added a little water until it was at least edible. Greg offered to go out and buy dinner instead. I said no. Buying an expensive meal wasn't the same as showing up.\n\nWhile we waited for the water to heat, Greg looked at the flowers he'd brought. The stems were bent and one bloom had lost half its petals. He started to put them into a vase, then stopped.\n\n\"I bought these because I thought maybe it'd make you less mad.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"That's not fair to you.\"\n\n\"No. But they can still be flowers.\"\n\nHe nodded. He found a chipped jar and filled it with clean water, then set the bouquet on the windowsill without expecting me to admire it. I watched him take the quiet answer seriously.\n\n\"Do you think I'm always going to mess up like this?\" he asked.\n\n\"I think we're both going to make mistakes. I don't think we have to keep making the same ones.\"\n\nHe sat down opposite me. His hair was damp from the rain, and there was a little smear of grease along his wrist where he'd helped with the neighbor's van.\n\n\"I thought if I kept helping,\" he said, \"you'd be proud of me.\"\n\n\"I'm proud that you helped. I'm upset you left me waiting without telling me. Those are two different things.\"\n\nHe let out a breath. \"I keep wanting them to cancel each other.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe wrote the revised plan on the back of the grocery list. I didn't want a promise that he'd never help someone again. I wanted him to notice when another request would cost somebody else the time he'd already agreed to give.\n\n\"What if I'm in the middle of a repair and my hands are full?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then send a message when it's safe. Tell me as soon as you reasonably can. Don't wait because you think you're nearly done.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And I can say that I'm disappointed without you making it a verdict on whether we're right for each other.\"\n\nHe looked down at the paper. \"Yeah. I need to work on that.\"\n\nThe pasta was still terrible. We ate it anyway. By the end of dinner, we'd stopped trying to find one sentence that would make everything all right.\n\nGreg reached toward my hand, paused and asked.\n\n\"Can I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe held it across the table. There was no need to kiss yet, and for the first time that evening neither of us was trying to hurry the other person into feeling better.\n\nWhile the sauce warmed, we made a practical plan. If he accepted a favor that would affect our agreed time, he'd send a message. If he couldn't safely stop to call, he'd tell me as soon as he could. If he needed to cancel, we'd decide whether to reschedule instead of leaving the evening hanging. I promised not to treat every late arrival as a verdict on how much he loved me.\n\n\"I have other partners, Greg,\" I said, not because he had forgotten but because it mattered to the plan. \"I've been clear that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I keep a calendar because the people I love shouldn't have to guess whether I'm coming. You deserve that from me too.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"I don't want to be the guy who makes plans impossible.\"\n\n\"Then start with one plan you can keep.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow breakfast?\"\n\n\"I have an early visit tomorrow.\"\n\nHe smiled ruefully. \"Okay. See, this is why I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Friday evening?\"\n\n\"Friday. I'll put it in writing.\"\n\nHe took a receipt from his pocket and wrote our agreed time on the back. Then he paused. \"Actually, I'll put it somewhere I won't accidentally wash it.\"\n\n\"The bar is rising.\"\n\nHe laughed for real.\n\nThe next morning a neighbor came by the car wash asking whether Greg could look at a faulty engine. He said he could inspect it after his booked work, not immediately. The neighbor went elsewhere for the emergency repair. Nobody died of disappointment.\n\nBy Wednesday, the neighbor had sent Greg three messages asking whether he'd reconsider working on the van. Greg showed me the phone with an expression somewhere between guilt and dread.\n\n“I could squeeze it in,” he said.\n\n“Would you want to?”\n\nHe opened his mouth and stopped. The question seemed to have surprised him more than the requests.\n\n“I'd like to help,” he said at last. “But I'm already booked at the car wash. And I promised Steven we'd talk on Thursday. If I promise one more thing, somebody loses.”\n\n“Then tell the neighbor what you can actually offer.”\n\nHe typed a message, erased it, typed another, and finally handed me the phone.\n\n“Too rude?”\n\nIt said that he couldn't do an emergency repair, but could recommend a mechanic with an opening. It was kind and clear. The neighbor replied with a thumbs-up and a thank-you.\n\nGreg looked offended by how simple it had been. “No dramatic argument. No speech about how I'm abandoning everybody.”\n\n“You'll have to write a song about the disappointment.”\n\n“I've got a title. Ballad of the Responsible Calendar. Nobody's buying tickets.”\n\nOn Thursday Steven called later than planned. Greg let the conversation run because his son needed him. He sent me a message before dinner to say he'd be a little late stopping by. The message wasn't poetic, but I felt myself loosen when it appeared.\n\nI liked that his apology wasn't perfect. It was becoming a habit of choosing a different action before the hurt could repeat.\n\n\nOn Friday, Greg arrived on time with a bag of vegetables and a determination not to touch the stove without instructions. The meal was better. We laughed about the first disaster, but we didn't pretend it hadn't hurt.\n\nWhen dinner was done, he reached across the table.\n\n\"Want me to hold your hand?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat there until I was the one who leaned in for a kiss. It was warm, familiar, and still had a little awkwardness in it. We closed the curtains later, with no audience and no need to perform an apology beyond what we'd actually changed.\n\nThe broken umbrella stayed by the door for a week. Then Greg fixed it, tested it in the rain, and gave it back to the neighbor he'd borrowed it from.\n\nI liked that he remembered a promise small enough not to become a song.","word_count":1485},"su-29":{"number":29,"title":"No Map for This","content":"The map was upside down.\n\nGreg denied this until the coastline began moving in the wrong direction, at which point he announced that he had discovered an exciting alternate route.\n\n\"An alternate route to where?\" I asked.\n\n\"Adventure.\"\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously like getting lost.\"\n\n\"It's only getting lost if you don't enjoy it.\"\n\nThe van rattled along a highway outside Beach City, its radio fading between songs. We'd left for a short weekend trip, planning to see a small music festival and return before Monday. I had packed food, medication, a charger and an actual paper itinerary. Greg had packed three guitar picks, two shirts and a bag of chips that he had already eaten.\n\nAt the first gas station, we discovered the festival had moved its opening set an hour earlier. Greg checked the flyer twice.\n\n\"That's odd. They told me they'd hold a place.\"\n\nI looked at him. \"You're performing?\"\n\n\"Maybe. I got invited. I was going to tell you.\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe grimaced. \"I know. I thought if I said it out loud, the trip would become about me being the old Mr. Universe again.\"\n\nI shut the glove compartment. I was annoyed, and also worried. The plan we'd made together was supposed to be a little breathing space. I didn't want to find out halfway there that he expected me to be part of a show.\n\n\"I'm glad somebody invited you,\" I said. \"I want you to be able to play. But I'm not a surprise backup singer.\"\n\n\"Right. No surprise backup singers.\"\n\n\"And I need to know the schedule before deciding whether I want to go.\"\n\nHe found a pencil. We wrote down the actual travel time, the festival's published hours and the nearest inexpensive place to sleep. I called the organizer from the station phone while Greg waited beside me.\n\nThey had space for him in an afternoon set, without any obligation to perform the old hits. If he wanted to play, he could confirm by noon. If not, the invitation would remain a friendly one.\n\n\"That's a real choice,\" I told him.\n\nHe looked at the flyer. \"Yeah. It is.\"\n\nWe stopped at a roadside diner because neither of us was in a state to make decisions on an empty stomach. The waitress gave us coffee that tasted like she was mad at somebody. Greg drank it cheerfully. I added enough sugar to qualify as structural repair.\n\n\"Are you disappointed?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little. I wanted a weekend that wasn't another performance.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\n\"You can love music and not want to be somebody's nostalgia act.\"\n\nHe stirred his coffee. \"I think part of me wanted them to remember me. Then I got scared they'd remember somebody I can't be anymore.\"\n\nI reached across the table, palm up. He took my hand.\n\n\"I'm here for the person who got the map upside down,\" I said. \"I don't need the old poster.\"\n\n\"Good, because the poster had terrible hair.\"\n\n\"You have terrible hair now.\"\n\n\"True. Consistency.\"\n\nWe called the organizer again and accepted the afternoon slot. Greg would play two new songs and one older one he actually wanted to perform. I would be in the audience if I chose. Nobody would list me as his duet partner without asking.\n\nThe organizer met us at the festival gate with a clipboard and an apology. The earlier schedule had been published before he checked with every performer; two musicians had already declined the new times.\n\n\"Can you still play the afternoon slot?\" he asked Greg.\n\n\"Yes. Two new songs, one old one. That's what we agreed.\"\n\nThe organizer nodded and corrected the sheet. He asked whether I wanted to help with the sound desk. I said I wanted to watch from the audience instead. The question didn't make him angry, and I was glad of it.\n\nWe had an hour before the set, so Greg and I walked to the little park beside the basketball court. A few teenagers were practicing chords on the steps. One recognized him from an old poster and asked whether he still played the same songs.\n\n\"Some of them,\" he said. \"You got any favorites?\"\n\nThe kid began a tune from a different band entirely. Greg laughed and offered one piece of advice about keeping the rhythm steady, then moved on rather than turning their afternoon into his performance.\n\n\"You're good with them,\" I said.\n\n\"I'm good with people who care about music more than whether somebody's famous.\"\n\nI considered that. We'd traveled to a new town with a real budget, a return date and other commitments waiting for us. I didn't need this weekend to prove our relationship was extraordinary.\n\n\"Could we go to that bookstore after you play?\" I asked.\n\n\"Absolutely. If it's still open.\"\n\n\"And if the concert runs late?\"\n\n\"Then we'll decide together. No surprise extra set.\"\n\nI smiled. We were both getting better at keeping promises small enough to be real. He took my hand as we walked back, the sound of somebody tuning a guitar drifting across the court.\n\n\"Thanks for coming,\" he said.\n\n\"I wanted to.\"\n\nHe looked almost startled by that simple answer, then squeezed my hand once.\n\nThe rest of the drive was quieter. We passed a field of tall grass and a little town where somebody had painted a mural across the side of a diner. Greg told me about a tour that had gone wrong years before, then let the story stay wrong without polishing it into a triumph.\n\nAt the motel, the air conditioner rattled every time it started. I checked the room's door and the route back to the parking lot. The bed was narrow, but the sheets were clean. We put the map on the dresser with the route facing the right direction.\n\n\"You know,\" Greg said, \"we could still turn around tomorrow.\"\n\n\"We could.\"\n\n\"Would you want to?\"\n\n\"I want to see the show. I also want to know we can leave if it stops being fun.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nOur relationship had room for other people and obligations. We had talked openly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory. The important part wasn't pretending we would always choose the same trip; it was asking rather than assuming.\n\nAt the park, the sound technician discovered that one of the outlets had no ground. She taped it off and asked the organizer to use the inspected circuit on the opposite wall.\n\nGreg saw the cable crossing the walkway and shook his head. “Hey, we can't leave that there. Someone's going to take a dive before I even get to the bad jokes.”\n\nHe helped reroute the lead behind the portable stage. It took ten minutes, and the organizer began looking at the clock.\n\n“We're already late,” she said.\n\n“Yeah,” Greg replied. “A late show beats an ambulance. Trust me, nobody's coming for the stunt work.”\n\nI had expected him to be resentful after the surprise booking conversation. Instead he asked whether the teenager from the park could borrow a chair for the opening song, and he waited while the stage manager checked where it would fit.\n\n“You didn't need me to run sound,” I told him.\n\n“Didn't want you to. You said you wanted to listen.” He shrugged. “I know, I know. A musician respecting an audience member's choices. They're gonna kick me out of the union.”\n\nI tugged him gently by the hem of his shirt. He turned, smiling, and for a second the noise of the crowd fell away. His hair was wind-tangled; he had a streak of grease on one wrist from helping with the cable stand.\n\n“You're very attractive when you don't volunteer my entire afternoon,” I said.\n\nHe blinked, then almost dropped his pick. “Whoa. Remind me to put that on a poster.”\n\n“Do not.”\n\nHe kissed the back of my hand, clumsy with the guitar balanced against his hip, and hurried toward the stage before the organizer had to call his name again.\n\n\nThe festival was small, with a stage beside an old basketball court and a volunteer checking the safety of the power cables. Greg played without introducing me as a prize or an answer to his past. He made an earnest joke about his hair. The audience groaned and applauded anyway.\n\nI sat on a folding chair near the side, cheering so loudly that he missed the first beat of the second song.\n\nAfterward he kissed me when I stepped close enough and asked.\n\nWe spent the evening walking through the town instead of staying for every act. There were cheap postcards and a bookstore with an enormous cat sleeping in the window. Greg bought me a postcard showing a road disappearing over a hill.\n\n\"To remember the great adventure,\" he said.\n\n\"To remember the map was upside down.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nIn the morning, he drew a crooked star near the diner on our map. Then he folded it properly and handed it to me.\n\nWe got home with money left for groceries, a song he'd enjoyed playing and enough stories to laugh about without pretending any of them had changed our whole lives.","word_count":1524},"su-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Brightest Room","content":"Greg volunteered both of us to repaint the community center.\n\nHe did it in front of six people and a woman holding a clipboard, which meant I couldn't even throw a paintbrush at him without witnesses.\n\n\"Both?\" I said.\n\nHe glanced sideways. \"I meant to ask.\"\n\n\"Before or after you gave away my Saturday?\"\n\n\"Somewhere in the middle.\"\n\nThe building had been dark long enough that the windows wore a film of dust. A section of the floor was taped off where the boards had lifted. There were chairs stacked in the hallway and old music stands leaning against a wall. Nobody knew whether the heating bill could be covered all winter.\n\nThe clipboard woman, who introduced herself as Pilar, handed us the budget. Volunteers had done what they could. A local donor offered to pay for new lights, but he wanted approval over the program and a say in who was allowed to perform.\n\n\"Sounds like a pretty big gift,\" Greg said.\n\n\"It's a purchase,\" I answered.\n\nPilar looked relieved that somebody had said it out loud.\n\nAt the next meeting, the donor arrived in a clean coat and spoke about restoring pride to the neighborhood. He wasn't unkind. He just wanted to put conditions on every dollar. Youth music nights could continue, he said, as long as performances stayed appropriate and the committee signed off on each artist.\n\n\"Who decides appropriate?\" I asked.\n\n\"The board.\"\n\n\"Who appoints the board?\"\n\n\"I would have a seat.\"\n\nGreg leaned back. He usually rescued awkward conversations with a joke, but this time he didn't.\n\n\"I was thirteen when somebody told me my music wasn't suitable for a serious stage,\" he said. \"They weren't wrong that it was loud. They were wrong that it meant I didn't deserve to play.\"\n\nThe donor folded his hands. \"You need funding.\"\n\n\"We do,\" Pilar said. \"But we need a community center even more.\"\n\nThe meeting ended without a donation. The lights stayed old for another week, and I spent most of the evening wondering whether we had done the right thing.\n\nGreg found me scrubbing paint from my fingernails.\n\n\"You think we blew it?\" he asked.\n\n\"I think I wanted money and didn't want the strings. I didn't like being made to choose.\"\n\n\"Me either.\"\n\nWe worked out a smaller plan with Pilar. Repair the safe rooms first. Open one evening a week. Keep the floor section closed until a qualified inspection. Publish the actual costs on the notice board, including the cost of paying the people doing skilled work. Greg offered a free performance but was careful not to promise somebody else's labor. I took charge of the volunteer schedule after asking each person what they could do.\n\nPilar's smaller plan came with a new problem: three people wanted the same practice room on the first evening, and all three had reasons that mattered.\n\nOne was a teenager saving for their own instrument. One was an older woman who had nowhere else to practice a violin without disturbing the neighbors. The third was a group whose drummer worked nights and could only come at a very particular hour.\n\nGreg looked at the schedule and rubbed his forehead. \"I thought the hard part was getting the lights on.\"\n\n\"That was a hard part,\" Pilar said. \"This one's another hard part.\"\n\nWe asked the musicians what time they actually needed instead of moving colored boxes around until the paper looked tidy. The violinist was happy to start early. The teenagers asked for a session where nobody watched them practice. The drummer could make the late slot if the room closed on time.\n\nI wrote the agreement on the board. Greg moved the amplifier away from the doorway after Pilar pointed out the cable was crossing the entrance. We borrowed a cord cover from the car wash, returned it at the end of the night and recorded the loan rather than leaving a mystery for the next volunteer.\n\nOne donor came by to see why we hadn't installed the expensive lights he'd offered.\n\n\"People will come if this place looks impressive,\" he said.\n\nPilar looked around at the ordinary bulbs, the patched chairs, and the small group of children tuning their instruments.\n\n\"People already came,\" she said.\n\n\"You're not thinking big enough.\"\n\n\"Maybe,\" she answered. \"But they can use this room tonight.\"\n\nHe left without agreeing to give money. Greg watched him go, then glanced at me.\n\n\"Do we have to feel noble about being broke?\"\n\n\"No. We can be annoyed. Then we can check whether the roof needs repairing.\"\n\nThe roof did need repairing. We added it to the list before ordering any more paint.\n\nLater a parent asked whether the teenagers could perform at the next open night. Greg crouched to their height and asked them, not their parent, whether they wanted an audience.\n\n\"Maybe later,\" one said.\n\n\"Cool,\" Greg replied. \"You can just learn a song first.\"\n\nI watched their shoulders relax.\n\nThere was nothing magical about the building. It needed wiring, money, honest staff hours and a room where saying no wouldn't mean losing your place. For the first time, I could imagine it lasting.\n\nOn the first night, three people showed up to play and two just came to listen. A teenager brought a badly tuned instrument, looking as though someone might laugh. Greg tuned it with them rather than taking it away and playing a dazzling demonstration.\n\nPilar rearranged the chairs when she noticed a narrow turning space beside the door. A volunteer moved the refreshment table out of the route. We didn't call it a heroic act. We just made the room usable.\n\nNear the end of the night, a gust shook the windows and the lights blinked. Everyone froze.\n\n\"Old wiring,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Old wiring we need checked,\" I corrected.\n\nPilar added it to the list. Pilar invited us back to see the electrician's written findings. The report was less exciting than the rumors that had already reached the coffee shop: a failed socket, an overloaded extension chain, and a section of flooring that needed inspection before heavy equipment could cross it.\n\nGreg read the sheet twice. “So the center's not haunted by angry electricity.”\n\n“It's haunted by deferred maintenance,” Pilar said.\n\n“Scariest ghost of all.”\n\nShe didn't laugh until he offered to help sort the safe cables by length. Then she handed him a bundle that had been tangled for so long it seemed to be growing roots.\n\nI sat at the table with the volunteer schedule. Three people had signed up for the new evening classes, but none of them had agreed to clean up after the late sessions. Pilar had been doing the work alone.\n\n“Can we ask for another shift?” I said.\n\n“We can ask,” she replied. “I'm not giving anyone extra hours by surprise.”\n\nGreg looked up from the cables, and I caught him noticing the word *ask*. The reminder could have become another speech about personal growth. Instead he held up an orange lead.\n\n“Is this one yours, Pilar?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Okay, good, because it's got the personality of seaweed and I'm not taking custody without paperwork.”\n\nShe laughed properly this time.\n\nWe left the damaged room closed, posted the electrician's next inspection date and put a real cleanup rota beside the music schedule. A volunteer crossed out an evening when she'd promised to visit family. No one asked her to justify it.\n\nThe center remained imperfect. It had people willing to return, which was a stronger reason to repair it than any donor's name above the stage.\n\n\nWe closed the back room until the electrician could inspect it. The concert moved to the safer side of the building, where the sound was smaller and the audience could still hear every word.\n\nAfter the last visitor left, Greg found me standing in the glow from the streetlamps.\n\n\"Hey,\" he said. \"About Saturday.\"\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I shouldn't have signed you up.\"\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck. \"I figured you'd want to help.\"\n\n\"I did want to help. I also wanted you to ask.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Next time, I ask.\"\n\n\"We can put that on the clipboard.\"\n\n\"Is there a punishment for repeat offenders?\"\n\n\"Sorting the tangled extension cords.\"\n\nHe winced. \"Cruel and unusual.\"\n\nWe laughed. He kissed my cheek, paused, and waited. I turned toward him and kissed him properly. Paint dried on my sleeve; the room still needed a lot of work. Outside, Beach City carried on being itself.\n\nI wasn't looking for a perfect building. I was looking for somewhere people could return without having to earn their place by pretending.\n\nThe following week, Pilar posted a revised schedule with a second volunteer lead. My name was on it for the nights I'd actually chosen. Greg's was on the music nights he could make. We each had lives outside the center, and that was part of what made the work sustainable.","word_count":1491},"su-33":{"number":33,"title":"A Door in the Wall","content":"The door looked like part of the wall.\n\nI'd seen it every time I went into the garage, but it had been painted the same faded blue as the paneling. It didn't have a handle, just a small latch hidden behind a shelf of spare parts.\n\nGreg found it while looking for a replacement fuse. He stared at the latch for so long I thought he'd forgotten what we'd come to do.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said. \"That room.\"\n\n\"What room?\"\n\n\"The storage room.\"\n\n\"I thought the storage room was the one with the lawn chairs.\"\n\n\"That's the other storage room.\"\n\n\"How many are there?\"\n\n\"Apparently one more than I remember.\"\n\nHe opened the door. The hinges protested, and a smell of cardboard and damp paper drifted out. We found a stack of boxes, an old suitcase with one broken clasp, and posters rolled into tubes. A small lightbulb hung from a wire near the ceiling, but Greg refused to turn it on until someone had inspected the aged socket.\n\n\"Good call,\" I said, switching on my flashlight.\n\n\"See? Responsible adult.\"\n\nWe moved the first box into the clean part of the garage. Greg opened it carefully. Inside were old music flyers, photographs and a letter addressed in handwriting he recognized before he said anything.\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n\"My parents.\"\n\nI didn't reach for the letter. \"Do you want to read it?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"We don't have to decide today.\"\n\nHe laughed once, without humor. \"It's been in a box for years. You'd think I'd have made up my mind.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to change your mind more than once.\"\n\nHe sat on the folding stool. I sat on a crate beside him, leaving enough space for him to put the envelope down. The afternoon sun caught dust in the doorway. Somewhere outside, somebody was washing a car with a radio turned up too loud.\n\nHe opened the letter eventually. I watched his face, not the words.\n\n\"They wanted me to come home,\" he said. \"But only if I was willing to be someone else when I got there.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell me more?\"\n\nHe showed me one sentence, then folded the page again. That was all I needed to read.\n\n\"I left because I couldn't make them proud without disappearing,\" he said. \"And now I keep thinking maybe I should have been more patient.\"\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said. \"Or maybe you were patient for a long time and leaving was the only thing you knew how to do.\"\n\nHe leaned back against the wall.\n\n\"I don't want to hate them forever.\"\n\n\"Then don't promise yourself that you have to. You also don't have to forgive them today.\"\n\nThe smallest box had been pushed behind an old amplifier. Greg picked it up and almost dropped it when the bottom sagged.\n\n\"Okay. That's a terrible box.\"\n\nI fetched a clean one from the garage and helped him move the contents: two concert tickets, a bundle of receipts from places that no longer existed, and a postcard with no message on the back.\n\n\"Do you want to keep those?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know. Some of them remind me of good nights. Some remind me of trying too hard to look happy.\"\n\n\"Then we can label them undecided.\"\n\nHe looked at me as though I'd suggested a new form of magic.\n\n\"That allowed?\"\n\n\"It's your storage room.\"\n\nHe grinned briefly. Then he found the family letter beneath the receipts and went quiet again.\n\nI didn't reach for it. There was a difference between helping somebody sort a room and demanding access to every secret hidden in its drawers.\n\n\"Would you mind if I put the letter somewhere dry?\" he asked.\n\n\"Not at all. Do you want me to read it?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe set it in a clean sleeve and placed it near the top of the box. The rest of the room could stay messy for another afternoon. The wiring still needed a qualified electrician before we used the old outlet.\n\nWhen we reached the doorway, he looked back.\n\n\"I keep thinking you won't understand why I want any of this.\"\n\n\"I don't have to understand every object to respect the person who wants to keep it.\"\n\nHe smiled awkwardly. \"That's kind of romantic for a box of bad memories.\"\n\n\"I contain multitudes. Mostly labeled in terrible handwriting.\"\n\nHe laughed, then leaned close enough that his shoulder touched mine.\n\n\"I know you have people you loved before me, and people you love now,\" he said. \"You've been honest about ethical non-monogamy and preferring polyamory. I guess I was scared you needed me to prove I was done with my past too.\"\n\n\"You don't. I want you here as you are, not with all the older rooms erased.\"\n\nHe took my hand. We shut the storeroom light off, left the door unlocked for the electrician's scheduled visit, and went to make food. It was enough for today.\n\nWe sorted the boxes slowly. Greg kept one absurd poster of himself with a hairstyle so ambitious I had to sit down laughing. He kept a scratched guitar strap and a small photograph of a concert where he'd been happy. He put the family letter into a sealed folder instead of burning it or hanging it above the bed.\n\nI pointed to the broken suitcase.\n\n\"Is that trash?\"\n\n\"I thought so.\"\n\nHe lifted it, found an old train ticket caught in the lining, and changed his mind.\n\n\"Maybe it can hold the stuff I'm not ready to decide about.\"\n\n\"That sounds useful.\"\n\nHe looked at me. \"You okay with it being here?\"\n\n\"Why wouldn't I be?\"\n\n\"Because the room is full of a life you weren't in.\"\n\n\"Greg, I have a life beyond this room too. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Loving someone doesn't mean acquiring the right to erase everybody who came before or everybody else who's here now.\"\n\nHe considered that, then smiled a little. \"I keep forgetting how sensible you are.\"\n\n\"That is a wildly inaccurate description.\"\n\n\"Still.\" He took my hand. \"Thanks for not making me choose between throwing this away and pretending it never hurt.\"\n\nWe carried a few salvageable boxes out to dry and left the wet ones on a plastic sheet for inspection. We didn't store anything against the old electrical outlet. Greg made a note to pay for an electrician before using the room, which was a distinctly unglamorous way to end a discovery.\n\nOne of the posters had a name scrawled across the back. Greg saw it and sat down on an upturned crate.\n\n“That's Marty’s handwriting,” he said.\n\n“The manager?”\n\n“Yeah. Old manager. Old mistakes. Probably a little of both.”\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across the edge of the cardboard, careful not to smear the ink. For a moment I thought he was going to tell me the entire history. Instead he folded the poster over and tucked it beneath the others.\n\n“You can keep it without explaining it to me,” I said.\n\n“That's kind of the thing,” he answered. “I want to explain some of it. I just don't want it to become a whole trial where I have to prove I was either a great guy or a complete idiot.”\n\nI shifted closer. The room smelled of wet paper, old amplifier dust and motor oil. There was nowhere dignified to sit, so I lowered myself onto the clean part of the floor.\n\n“Maybe you were both,” I said.\n\nHe made a face. “Gee, thanks.”\n\n“Not at the same time. Possibly.”\n\n“Better.”\n\nHe told me about a night when he'd been sure a bad contract was the only way to get a band on the road, how he'd signed before reading everything because he was afraid of being told no. He didn't blame Rose for the choices he made before he met her, and he didn't turn his old manager into an excuse for everything that followed.\n\n“I wish somebody'd told me I could walk away,” he said.\n\n“Would you have listened?”\n\n“Absolutely not.” He grinned, but it faded. “Maybe later.”\n\nWe agreed to photograph the poster before deciding whether to keep it. The photo would be private. Greg didn't owe the wall a shrine, and he didn't owe me the inside of every memory.\n\nAs I stood, my hand slipped on the crate. He caught my elbow, then immediately let go when I had my balance.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“It's okay. The floor's not my finest dance partner.”\n\n“It's got two left feet,” he said. “And somehow, both of them are mine.”\n\nI laughed, and he laughed too. We left the old papers where they could be sorted on a day when neither of us was tired of being brave.\n\n\nAs evening came, the shadows stretched across the garage. He stood in the doorway with the letter folder in his hand.\n\n\"Do you want some company tonight?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe went into the house and made sandwiches. He told me a story about the first poster he'd hung as a teenager, and how he'd been certain it would make him famous. I told him I once kept a ridiculous souvenir because I couldn't bear to admit the trip had been a disappointment. We laughed about being younger and less certain.\n\nHe leaned close but stopped.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nIt wasn't a cure for the letter. It wasn't supposed to be. His mouth was warm, his hand a little shaky against my cheek. I moved closer, and he let out a breath he seemed to have been holding for years.\n\nLater, we closed the curtains and let the evening be ours. The storage-room door remained open, its light switched off, with the boxes labeled for another day.\n\nThe next morning Greg added a second note to the electrician's list: *Ask before moving stored papers.* He showed it to me without making a speech.\n\nI liked that more than a promise to forget.","word_count":1666},"su-36":{"number":36,"title":"When We Choose Each Other","content":"The string lights were crooked.\n\nOne loop dipped low enough to brush Greg's hair. Another had been wound around the same post three times because someone had changed their mind about where the party ought to end. The band was late, the paper plates were trying to escape in the wind, and Greg was standing in the middle of the Beach City square with a microphone he had no intention of using.\n\n\"You're staring,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm assessing the danger.\"\n\n\"The lights?\"\n\n\"Your haircut.\"\n\nHe grinned and tucked a loose strand behind his ear.\n\nThe celebration had started as a small gathering after a community cleanup. People had carried away storm debris, repaired the picnic tables, and borrowed the church's folding chairs for the evening. Greg had volunteered music for one set; I had promised to help lay out the food. Neither of us had agreed to be the event's romantic headline.\n\nThen a local newspaper had asked for a photograph.\n\nThe reporter, a young woman named Ellis, approached with a camera and a folded page of interview notes. She looked nervous.\n\n\"Greg, can I get one shot of you and Jasper? People have been talking about you two.\"\n\nGreg's smile thinned, almost invisibly.\n\n\"Depends what the story's about,\" he said.\n\n\"The cleanup. And, well, the relationship. It's kind of sweet. Maybe a little unconventional.\"\n\nI set down a stack of cups.\n\n\"Unconventional how?\"\n\nEllis glanced at her notes.\n\n\"Someone said you travel a lot, Jasper. That you see other people.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, not to make me answer but to check whether I wanted to.\n\n\"I do,\" I said. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Everyone I'm involved with knows that, and each relationship is based on informed choice.\"\n\nEllis nodded quickly.\n\n\"So you aren't serious with Greg?\"\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\n\"That's not what I said.\"\n\nShe blushed.\n\n\"Sorry. I didn't mean—\"\n\n\"I know. But I'd like you to ask what we actually mean instead of choosing a story where somebody must be temporary.\"\n\nGreg exhaled.\n\n\"I was about to say something stupid,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What kind?\"\n\n\"Something about being lucky to have whatever time you can spare for me.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"I know. It sounds kind of selfless until you realize it makes me look like a guy standing around hoping you'll pick him.\"\n\nEllis lowered her camera.\n\n\"I can leave the relationship out of the article.\"\n\n\"That would be my preference,\" I said. \"You can write about the cleanup. Ask the people who worked on the drainage ditch. Ask who hauled the chairs. Don't turn our private time into a test for strangers.\"\n\nEllis nodded.\n\nThen she asked, \"Can I photograph the repaired picnic table?\"\n\n\"Please,\" I said.\n\nShe went to find the volunteers.\n\nFor a moment neither Greg nor I spoke. The wind lifted the napkins, and we had to chase them down the path in a ridiculous little scramble.\n\nGreg caught the last one against his chest.\n\n\"Great start,\" he said.\n\n\"You're very dignified.\"\n\n\"I've been told.\"\n\nWe returned to the tables. He straightened the napkins while I tried to decide whether to bring up the line he'd almost said. I didn't want to leave it sitting between us like a broken instrument neither of us wished to touch.\n\n\"About being grateful for whatever time I can spare,\" I said.\n\nHe went still.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you actually feel that way?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" He looked at his hands. \"I don't want to make you smaller. I like that you have places to go and people to see. I also don't want to pretend I never miss you.\"\n\n\"Then tell me when you miss me. That's allowed.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"Yes. I'm your partner, not a holiday rental.\"\n\nHe laughed, startled.\n\n\"Okay. That's fair.\"\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I choose you when I'm here. I also choose to be honest when I have plans elsewhere. Those aren't competing truths.\"\n\nHe set the stack of napkins down.\n\n\"And I can ask for time?\"\n\n\"You can. I can say yes or no. You get to say no to me too.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an expression both embarrassed and intensely fond.\n\n\"Pretty sure I'm going to need practice.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nSteven arrived carrying a cooler, rescued the conversation by tripping over a cable, and promptly asked why the band hadn't started yet.\n\n\"Artistic suspense,\" Greg said.\n\n\"You're not even playing yet.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nSteven shook his head and helped set the cooler down. He had brought enough cold drinks for the volunteers. Greg asked whether he wanted to stay for the music; Steven said yes, then went to help move chairs.\n\nNo one turned him into a witness to our romantic discussion.\n\nA little later Ellis returned with the article draft. She had removed the guesses about our relationship and added the names of the volunteers who had repaired the square. She asked us to check our direct quotations before printing.\n\n\"Better,\" I said.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Look, someone actually wrote about the storm drain. This town is growing up.\"\n\nThe band finally arrived. Their drummer apologized for being late; a delivery truck had blocked the road. Greg helped carry the cases but did not volunteer to manage the whole set. We hung the last string of lights high enough for people to walk beneath without ducking.\n\nWhen the first song began, Greg reached for my hand.\n\n\"Want to dance?\"\n\n\"I thought you'd never ask.\"\n\n\"I'm trying this new thing called asking.\"\n\n\"Very advanced.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me into the uneven little space between the tables. We moved badly at first, bumping shoulders and laughing when the rhythm changed. He didn't pull me closer until I did, and when his thumb brushed my knuckles I felt the warmth of the choice we'd made in front of nobody who needed to know.\n\nAcross the square, the lights shone on the volunteers, the repaired tables, and the people eating too much cake. There was no grand declaration. We didn't ask the band to play a song about us.\n\nBefore we rejoined the crowd, Greg found a quiet place beside the stacked chairs where the music was just a muffled beat through the wall.\n\n“You look nice,” he said.\n\n“You've been looking at me all night.”\n\n“Yeah, but I was trying to decide whether saying it would make me sound like a guy who just discovered compliments.”\n\n“It might.”\n\nHe gave me an embarrassed grin. “Well, then. You look *really* nice.”\n\nI put my palm against his chest. His shirt was warm from setting up amplifiers, and beneath it I felt the quick, startled rhythm of his breath. I liked that he wasn't always smooth. He'd spent years singing to strangers and could still be thrown off balance by one person standing too close.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” I asked.\n\nGreg's smile vanished for a second. Not because he didn't want it. Because he was listening.\n\n“Yeah,” he said softly. “Please.”\n\nI kissed him beneath the crooked string lights. He made a little noise of surprise when I touched the back of his neck, then rested both hands carefully at my waist. There was nothing dramatic about the way we stood together, and yet I could feel how much effort it cost him not to turn tenderness into a joke.\n\nWhen we parted, he rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“Uh,” he said. “I had a very cool thing to say.”\n\n“Did you?”\n\n“Completely gone. I think you broke the lead singer.”\n\n“Can he be repaired?”\n\n“Maybe. Might need another kiss. No promises about the warranty.”\n\nI laughed against his cheek. We stayed another moment until Pilar called for a hand with the chairs. Greg asked if I wanted to return with him, and I did. Later we would close the curtains on the part of the evening that belonged to us. For now, he had volunteered for a cleanup shift, and I didn't mind watching him keep it.\n\n\nWhen Greg kissed me, it was brief and sweet. I leaned against him, and he rested his cheek against my hair.\n\n\"Can we keep doing this?\" he asked.\n\n\"The terrible dancing?\"\n\n\"That, yes. But I meant being honest before I start making jokes instead.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Let's practice.\"\n\nWe left the square after the cleanup shift we'd agreed to, not after every person had gone home. Steven promised to help the last volunteers collect the chairs. Ellis gave us a copy of the revised article, now mostly about the town's flood drains and the people who fixed them.\n\nGreg tucked it under his arm.\n\nAt the van, he stopped and looked back at the crooked lights.\n\n\"Should we fix that loop?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Really.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nThe next morning we returned with a ladder and a proper set of clips. The lights hung straight for exactly one afternoon before the sea breeze twisted one of them sideways again.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThe crooked loop stayed until the next scheduled repair, and our relationship did not depend on the entire town approving the shape of it.","word_count":1521},"su-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Song We Keep","content":"Greg had written the last line of the song three times.\n\nOn the first scrap of paper, it promised a forever neither of us had asked for.\n\nOn the second, it sounded like somebody leaving before they could be left.\n\nOn the third, the line stopped halfway through.\n\nHe put all three beside his guitar and stared at them.\n\n\"I think I've invented a new kind of writer's block,\" he announced.\n\n\"What's it called?\"\n\n\"Having feelings and being annoying about them.\"\n\nI laughed, then saw he wasn't quite joking.\n\nWe were in the garage, surrounded by cables, a small recording device, and a box of things Greg had been meaning to sort for months. The air smelled of warm dust and sea salt. One wall held posters from shows I'd never attended; another held a shelf with spare strings and the battered notebook where he kept chord progressions.\n\nThe song had begun beside Starwake Pier, where water made a low note beneath the wooden boards. Greg had copied part of that rhythm into the melody, leaving a long pause after the fifth note. He liked the pause when he played alone. Every time he tried to finish the lyrics, he filled it with a promise too large to trust.\n\n\"Play the beginning again?\" I asked.\n\nHe picked up the guitar.\n\nThe opening was gentle and slightly crooked. I knew the melody well enough to hum along, though I still missed the turn in the third phrase.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"That's the wrong harmony.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\n\"No, wait. Do it again.\"\n\nI hummed.\n\nHe listened, then picked out a new chord beneath it.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said.\n\n\"Good oh?\"\n\n\"Interesting oh. That's the kind that gets you in trouble.\"\n\nHe played it again. The new chord made the next note sound uncertain, almost as though the tune had reached a doorway and didn't know whether to step through.\n\n\"Keep that,\" I said.\n\nGreg set down the guitar.\n\n\"You're not just saying that to be nice?\"\n\n\"No. I actually like the way it doesn't resolve.\"\n\nHe stared at the unfinished line.\n\n\"That's the problem.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"If I finish it,\" he said, \"people might think I'm promising something about us. Like the song's a contract.\"\n\n\"Do you think that?\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry I'll start believing it.\"\n\nHe looked embarrassed, and I knew how much it had cost him to admit it. Greg was generous with laughter. Fear was harder. He tended to pack it inside a joke and hope the music would do the rest.\n\nI sat on the overturned crate beside him.\n\n\"We can write a song without making it a binding agreement.\"\n\n\"Sure. But then I get scared that if it's not a big promise, it doesn't mean enough.\"\n\nI thought about the green notebook in my bag, the one with my living agreements and the things I was learning to ask for.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without turning it into a verse?\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\n\"I love you. I am ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory, and we've already talked about that honestly. I don't want the song to erase those choices. I don't want it to make you a consolation prize, either.\"\n\nGreg looked down at his hands.\n\n\"That's the part I'm bad at.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"Believing I matter without being the only one.\"\n\nI felt my chest tighten.\n\n\"I can't prove you matter by making somebody else disappear.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But I can tell you why you're here with me. Because I like your terrible jokes. Because you listen when I say a plan isn't working. Because you remember what I drink when I get distracted. Because you can be scared and still choose to tell me.\"\n\nHe breathed out slowly.\n\n\"That's a pretty good list.\"\n\n\"There's also the van.\"\n\n\"Ah. The real reason.\"\n\n\"The seats are surprisingly comfortable.\"\n\nHe laughed, and it eased something in the room.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"Can I ask you for a date next week?\"\n\n\"Yes. I have an afternoon free after Moonroot.\"\n\n\"Just us?\"\n\n\"Just us. If the archive runs late, I'll send word instead of leaving you guessing.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nWe sat for a while, listening to the little recording device hum.\n\nThen I picked up the third page.\n\n\"What if the last line isn't about forever? What if it's about something we keep doing, one time at a time?\"\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Like what?\"\n\n\"Listening.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Too much like a public service announcement.\"\n\n\"Coming back?\"\n\n\"Too much like a ferry schedule.\"\n\nI grinned.\n\n\"You're impossible.\"\n\n\"Artistically sensitive.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\nHe played the melody again. This time he left the space open, and I hummed my wrong harmony over the next chord. It didn't resolve neatly. It sounded like two people who had heard each other and still had different ways of reaching the same place.\n\nGreg's eyes lit up.\n\n\"That's it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"We don't need another line.\"\n\n\"Seriously?\"\n\n\"The space is the line.\"\n\nI stared.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"That's either brilliant or you've avoided writing words so successfully you've declared silence a lyric.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nWe tested the song on the little recording device. The first take had a creaking chair in the middle of the pause. The second caught the sound of a truck backing up outside. On the third, Greg missed a chord because I'd made him laugh.\n\nWe kept the third.\n\nIt sounded like us.\n\nLater we took the guitar down to the pier. The boards were dry, the safety notices were posted, and the wind carried the smell of the ocean through the railings. Greg checked the stage schedule with the volunteer steward before setting up. We weren't there to take over a public performance space; we wanted five minutes to hear how the new chord sounded outdoors.\n\nA young busker was already playing near the bench.\n\n\"Mind if we wait?\" Greg asked.\n\nThe musician shook their head.\n\n\"Not at all. I've got ten minutes left.\"\n\nWe listened until their set finished, then helped move the stand to storage. Greg played our song for the open air.\n\nThe last phrase hung between the sea and the wooden railing.\n\nNo triumphant resolution.\n\nNo tragic ending.\n\nA breath.\n\nI leaned against the post.\n\n\"What do you think?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the page, then at me.\n\n\"I think I'm done trying to make this song answer questions it wasn't asked.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That's a good place to stop.\"\n\nHe set the guitar in its case.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me warmly, one hand against my cheek, and pulled back with a laugh when a gust of wind blew his hair into my face.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"I was aiming for majestic.\"\n\n\"You hit ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Close enough.\"\n\nOn the way home, Greg stopped at the empty rehearsal hall to return a borrowed microphone stand.\n\nThe caretaker was stacking chairs. She glanced at the notebook under Greg's arm.\n\n“Finished your big song?”\n\n“Mostly,” Greg said. “I left a space at the end.”\n\n“Can't sell a space on a record.”\n\n“Good thing I'm not selling one tonight.”\n\nThe caretaker shrugged and carried on. Greg looked almost embarrassed by how much the exchange had mattered.\n\n“Old me would've rewritten the whole thing before she could ask again,” he admitted.\n\nI shifted the guitar case against my hip. “Old you might've had a completely different song to write.”\n\n“Yeah. He was kind of a mess.”\n\n“He was also a musician.”\n\nGreg looked at me, then back at the door. “I think I keep trying to apologize for him and make sure he gets a standing ovation. Both at once. Not super efficient.”\n\n“No. But it's an impressive workload.”\n\nHe laughed. We walked the rest of the way slowly. The boardwalk smelled of fried dough and salt; someone had left a paper cup beneath the bench. Greg stopped to pick it up and put it in the bin without making it a gesture worth announcing.\n\nAt the van, he asked whether I wanted to hear the last verse again. I did. He played it quietly, and the unfinished space arrived where it belonged. We listened to the sea move beneath the melody.\n\n“I used to think every song needed to explain why somebody stayed,” he said.\n\n“Does this one?”\n\nHe shook his head. “I think it just needs to remember we both got to choose.”\n\nI leaned close and kissed the corner of his mouth before he could make a joke out of the sentence. He smiled, the guitar resting against his knee, and I could feel the laughter he was holding back.\n\nIt wasn't an answer to every question. It was a song with room for the next thing that happened.\n\n\nAt home, we put the third page in the song notebook. We kept the earlier drafts, too, because they were part of how we'd reached this version. Greg wrote a small note beside the empty final line:\n\n*Leave room for the next day.*\n\nThat evening we ate dinner from paper cartons and watched the light move across the garage floor. The rest of our time stayed private; no lyric, no performance, no audience.\n\nIn the morning, Greg played the unfinished song once more.\n\nHe stopped at the open space.\n\nThis time he smiled before the next chord.\n\nIt was finished enough to keep living.","word_count":1559},"su-41":{"number":41,"title":"A Song That Isn't a Promise","content":"Greg had written a song about a door.\n\nThat should not have surprised me.\n\nHe wrote songs about the ocean, broken-down cars, coffee that had gone cold, and once about a chair that was so uncomfortable it deserved a public apology. The door was part of our history now. So was the patched blanket folded in the back of his van, and the fact that we kept leaving places and coming back without treating every departure as the end.\n\nThe song was good.\n\nThat was the problem.\n\n\"Play the second verse again,\" I said.\n\nGreg looked up from his guitar.\n\n\"I've done that three times.\"\n\n\"Four, if you count the version where you forgot the words.\"\n\n\"I was improvising.\"\n\n\"You rhymed door with floor.\"\n\n\"Classic songwriting technique.\"\n\nHe grinned and strummed the opening chord again. We were sitting on Starwake Pier beneath a string of lanterns, with the evening sea turning copper around the pilings. At the far end, two children were trying to teach a wooden toy boat to sail against the wind.\n\nGreg began to sing.\n\nThe melody was warm and easy to remember. It reminded me of Beach City in the hour before sunset, when the boardwalk noise softened and the ocean seemed bigger than everything people had been worrying about.\n\nThen he reached the line that made me look up.\n\nOne door, one heart, one place to stay.\n\nHe finished the verse and waited.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"It's pretty.\"\n\n\"Pretty good or pretty bad?\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHis smile faded.\n\n\"Okay. Talk to me.\"\n\nI rested my hands on the edge of the bench.\n\n\"Does that line mean what it sounds like?\"\n\nHe looked down at the page.\n\n\"It's a song about home.\"\n\n\"I know. But it sounds like the kind of promise we talked about not making.\"\n\nHe read the line again. The pause went on long enough that a wave slapped the pier below us.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said.\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\n\"I didn't mean it like that.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nHe ran his thumb along the guitar's edge.\n\n\"Somebody at the car wash heard the first version and said it was romantic that I'd finally found the one person I needed.\"\n\nI felt something cold settle behind my ribs.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Not because I agreed,\" he said quickly. \"I just didn't know what to say in front of the customers. And then it felt weird to correct it later.\"\n\n\"That's fair. It can be awkward.\"\n\nHe nodded, but I could see he was still waiting for the harder part.\n\n\"I don't want the story people tell about us to be that I changed my mind about polyamory because you were special enough,\" I said. \"You *are* special to me. That's not what the word exclusive means.\"\n\nGreg set the guitar down between his feet.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\nHe looked toward the water.\n\n\"I think I know in my head. Sometimes my heart takes the scenic route.\"\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\n\"Well, it has a van.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"I've spent years telling myself it was okay when people left,\" he said. \"Sometimes it was. Sometimes it wasn't. I don't always know the difference until afterward. I don't want to turn that into a rule that keeps you from loving the way you love.\"\n\nI moved a little closer.\n\n\"You can still ask for reassurance.\"\n\n\"Then I want some.\" He swallowed. \"If you go through another door, do you still want to come back and play terrible songs with me?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even if I make you listen to the chair song again?\"\n\n\"That's pushing it.\"\n\nHe laughed, and the tension shifted.\n\n\"But yes,\" I said. \"I do. And I want us to keep making agreements instead of guessing. If something changes, we tell each other. If you want a date that's just ours, say so. If you need space, say that too.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"And no letting rumors do the talking for us.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nGreg picked up his pencil and crossed out the line.\n\n\"How about 'a door that opens, a light that stays'?\"\n\n\"Better.\"\n\n\"Still too sentimental?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"Great. That's the brand.\"\n\nWe tried the new verse. He stumbled over the rhythm twice. I caught the beat with my hands on the bench, and he adjusted the melody until the words sounded like something he actually meant.\n\nA little later, Iroh appeared with two cups of tea and an expression of mild curiosity.\n\n\"Are you rehearsing?\" he asked.\n\n\"Negotiating with a chord,\" Greg said.\n\n\"A difficult task. Chords are often very stubborn.\"\n\n\"Finally, somebody understands.\"\n\nIroh set the cups on the bench and listened to a verse. He did not offer a verdict. He asked what Greg wanted the song to carry.\n\n\"The feeling of having somewhere I can go back to,\" Greg said. \"Without saying it has to be the only place anybody goes.\"\n\nIroh thought about that.\n\n\"Then perhaps the melody should leave room at the end.\"\n\nGreg considered the unfinished measure.\n\n\"That's actually good advice.\"\n\nI pointed at Iroh. \"Don't tell him. He'll start charging by the metaphor.\"\n\nIroh's smile deepened.\n\nThe tea warmed my hands. The lanterns turned on above us, one after another. People from the square began to gather near the little stage, drawn by the music without having been promised a performance.\n\nGreg looked at them, then at me.\n\n\"Want to hear it all the way through?\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nHe played.\n\nThis time, when he reached the new line, his voice steadied. The song did not claim that one person could replace everyone a heart had ever known. It was about the courage of making a place, keeping a light and coming back because you wanted to.\n\nWhen he finished, a few people clapped. One of the children shouted that the song needed more frogs.\n\nGreg took the criticism seriously.\n\n\"That's what the bridge is for,\" he told them.\n\nThe children cheered.\n\nThe community notice board had acquired a hand-lettered announcement while we were playing. Someone had written Greg's name in enormous letters and added that the concert was for “Jasper's one true love.”\n\nI stared at the sign.\n\nGreg followed my eyes and winced. “Oh. That's... not great.”\n\nThe volunteer who'd made it came over with a staple gun. “It's supposed to be sweet!”\n\n“I know,” Greg said, not unkindly. “But Jasper didn't ask to have their relationships summed up on a poster. And I didn't ask to be somebody's grand prize.”\n\nI didn't need him to make a speech for me. I did appreciate that he had heard me say this before and remembered it now.\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous,” I explained to the volunteer. “I prefer polyamory. Everybody I'm with knows that, and we talk about what we're comfortable sharing. I'd rather the sign advertise the music.”\n\nShe looked mortified. “I didn't mean to offend anyone.”\n\n“I know,” I said. “Could we change it?”\n\nWe took the paper down and wrote a new one together. It listed the musicians, the start time and the fact that the western pier remained closed until repairs were complete. None of the names were placed above anyone else's.\n\nGreg stood back to look at it. “Aw, man. Now it looks professional. People are gonna expect me to know what I'm doing.”\n\n“You do know what you're doing.”\n\n“Don't spread that around. Terrible for my image.”\n\nThe volunteer laughed and went to help with the last chairs. Greg tucked his arm around me only after I leaned closer.\n\n“Thanks,” I said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Being willing to change a sign without turning the whole town into a referendum on us.”\n\n“I'm still gonna campaign for better stage lighting,” he said.\n\n“That seems fair.”\n\nHe smiled into my hair. We had a date to get home for, and for once neither of us had promised our evening away to anybody else.\n\n\nAfter the chairs were folded and the cups returned, we walked toward the Starwake Door with the guitar between us. A breeze lifted the edge of my sweater. Greg reached for my hand and waited until I took it.\n\n\"You know,\" he said, \"it's still pretty romantic.\"\n\n\"The song?\"\n\n\"The part where nobody has to lie.\"\n\nI stopped beside the door. The brass latch was cool beneath my free hand. He turned to face me, his smile a little nervous.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHis kiss was warm and slightly clumsy because the guitar kept bumping his leg. I leaned closer, laughing into his shoulder. He kissed me again, softer.\n\nWe did not need the pier to applaud.\n\nBack in Beach City, the van waited under the streetlamp with the patched blanket folded where we'd left it. Greg asked whether I wanted to stay for a while.\n\nI told him yes.\n\nHe opened the door. The rest of the evening belonged to us, not to the lyrics on a page.\n\nIn the morning, a small folded note sat on the dashboard.\n\nNEW SONG RULE: CHECK WITH JASPER BEFORE PROMISING ANYTHING TO THE WHOLE UNIVERSE.\n\nUnderneath, he had added a little drawing of the world's worst chair.\n\nI kept the note.","word_count":1528}}};for(const s of library.series){const byId=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=byId[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}s.total_word_count=s.chapters.reduce((sum,ch)=>sum+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
